<section class="page-head">
<div class="sigil-lg" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><path d="M20 8 L32 20 L20 32 L8 20 Z"/><circle cx="20" cy="20" r="4.5"/><circle cx="20" cy="20" r="1.6" style="fill:currentColor;stroke:none"/></svg></div>
<div>
<h1>The Looking Studio</h1>
<p class="sub">One visual habit, twelve ways of looking</p>
</div>
</section>

<main class="reading">
<p class="lede">Look first, with nothing marked. Then, if you want help, turn on a reversible guide and see whether the relation was already there. Six lenses start from painting, six from sculpture — twelve starting points, not a sequence.</p>
<p class="page-note">The created comparison images in every lens are original studies made with OpenAI GPT Image 2 through Codex, built to isolate one visual relationship at a time. Historical artworks and documented photographs are separately identified and credited where they appear.</p>

<div class="orient">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">How to look</span><em>One habit, before the lenses</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Look at both arrangements. In each one, which shapes seem grouped together, and which remain separate? What visible relationship creates that impression?</p>
<div class="guide-unit" id="unit-look">
<div class="orient-pair">
<figure class="arr scene"><figcaption>Arrangement A</figcaption><svg viewBox="0 0 320 240" role="img" aria-label="A tall vertical form and an open arc, with a circle sitting outside the arc, separated by a clear gap."><line class="ink" x1="52" y1="42" x2="52" y2="198"/><path class="ink" d="M150 198 Q214 58 278 198"/><circle class="solid" cx="288" cy="70" r="17"/></svg><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 320 240" aria-hidden="true"><line class="guide-line" x1="278" y1="90" x2="288" y2="90"/><circle class="marker" cx="283" cy="90" r="11"/><text class="marker-text" x="283" y="90" style="font-size:15px">1</text></svg></figure>
<figure class="arr scene"><figcaption>Arrangement B</figcaption><svg viewBox="0 0 320 240" role="img" aria-label="The same tall vertical form and open arc, with the same circle now sitting inside the arc's opening."><line class="ink" x1="52" y1="42" x2="52" y2="198"/><path class="ink" d="M150 198 Q214 58 278 198"/><circle class="solid" cx="214" cy="162" r="26"/></svg><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 320 240" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-shape" d="M162 182 Q214 104 266 182 L266 198 L162 198 Z"/><circle class="marker" cx="214" cy="162" r="11"/><text class="marker-text" x="214" y="162" style="font-size:15px">2</text></svg></figure>
</div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p>In Arrangement A, <strong>1 an empty interval</strong> keeps the circle outside the arc. In Arrangement B, <strong>2 the same-sized circle</strong> sits inside the arc's opening. Changing a visible relationship — nothing else — can change how strongly parts appear grouped.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>Artists shape a viewer's experience by arranging relationships. Before asking what a work means, notice which parts appear grouped or separated — and what visible choices create that effect.</p></div></div>
</div>
</div>

