<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="space" style="--acc:#75435B;--acc-line:#75435B;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#75435B 12%,white)">
<section class="lens" id="lens-space" data-screen-label="painting-space">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><line x1="3" y1="34" x2="37" y2="34"/><circle cx="17" cy="11" r="3.5"/><path d="M17 14.5 L 17 30 M 17 19 L 13 25 M 17 19 L 21 25 M 17 30 L 14 34 M 17 30 L 20 34"/><circle cx="30" cy="22" r="2"/><path d="M30 24 L 30 31 M 30 26.5 L 28 30 M 30 26.5 L 32 30"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">01 · Painting</span><h2 class="lens-title">Space: Where does the picture place you?</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">A painting is flat, but an artist can arrange forms so your eye stays at its surface — or feels invited into a pictured space.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Created illustration</span><em>One arrangement · two spatial relationships</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Where do you feel positioned in relation to the objects? Which parts seem near or far, and what makes them feel that way?</p>
<div class="guide-unit" id="unit-space">
<div class="pair">
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:1672/941;background:linear-gradient(180deg,#3a3733,#221f1c)" role="img" aria-label="A pale panel, terracotta arch, and orange sphere sit separately, with open intervals between them."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="obj" style="position:absolute;width:18%;left:10%;bottom:12%;height:auto" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/painting-space-panel.png" alt="" /><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="obj" style="position:absolute;width:28%;left:40%;bottom:12%;height:auto;z-index:2" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/painting-space-arch.png" alt="" /><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="obj" style="position:absolute;width:11%;left:77%;bottom:13%;height:auto" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/painting-space-sphere.png" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1000 562" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M283 385 L397 385 M682 385 L770 385"/><circle class="marker" cx="340" cy="385" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="340" y="385">1</text></svg></div>
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:1672/941;background:linear-gradient(180deg,#3a3733,#221f1c)" role="img" aria-label="The same panel, arch, and sphere now overlap, with pale floor lines receding beneath them."><svg class="floor" viewBox="0 0 1000 562" style="position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M65 520 L945 520 M142 476 L883 476 M224 438 L809 438 M315 410 L735 410" fill="none" stroke="rgba(245,232,207,.45)" stroke-width="2"/></svg><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="obj" style="position:absolute;width:18%;left:31%;bottom:12%;height:auto;z-index:3" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/painting-space-panel.png" alt="" /><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="obj" style="position:absolute;width:28%;left:40%;bottom:12%;height:auto;z-index:2" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/painting-space-arch.png" alt="" /><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" class="obj" style="position:absolute;width:11%;left:77%;bottom:13%;height:auto;z-index:4" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/painting-space-sphere.png" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1000 562" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M490 170 L490 475"/><circle class="marker" cx="490" cy="280" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="490" y="280">2</text><path class="guide-line" d="M175 562 L625 390 M860 562 L625 390"/><circle class="marker" cx="625" cy="390" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="625" y="390">3</text></svg></div>
</div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>1 Open intervals</strong> keep the panel, arch, and sphere distinct on the left. On the right, <strong>2 the panel's edge overlaps the arch</strong> and <strong>3 floor seams narrow</strong> toward one area. These cues can make some forms feel nearer or farther away and can suggest a threshold into pictured space.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>Placement, overlap, scale, and converging lines can construct spatial relationships, affecting where the viewer seems to stand and how the eye moves through the picture.</p></div></div>
<section class="realart" data-screen-label="painting-space-realart">
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3><em>Paris Street; Rainy Day</em></h3><p class="work-meta">Gustave Caillebotte · 1877 · oil on canvas</p></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Look at the street, the figures, and the building corners. What makes some parts of the city feel near to you and other parts feel farther away?</p>
<div class="guide-unit"><div class="scene natural" data-hist role="img" aria-label="Gustave Caillebotte's Paris Street; Rainy Day: a couple with umbrellas in the foreground while streets, pedestrians, carriages, and buildings recede behind them."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/hist-caillebotte.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 2500 1941" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-zone" d="M1210 736 C1390 628 1680 655 1860 818 L1980 1360 L1830 1810 L1320 1810 L1225 1350 Z"/><path class="guide-zone" d="M435 700 C520 654 702 672 788 747 L748 884 C639 920 502 905 434 840 Z"/><path class="guide-line" d="M765 790 L370 1195"/><path class="guide-line" d="M765 790 L1125 1940"/><path class="guide-line" d="M900 720 L765 790"/><circle class="marker" cx="1580" cy="1240" r="60"/><text class="marker-text" x="1580" y="1240">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="925" cy="695" r="60"/><text class="marker-text" x="925" y="695">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="475" cy="650" r="60"/><text class="marker-text" x="475" y="650">3</text></svg></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden id="cap-space-ra"><span class="cap-label">Look again at the whole painting</span><p>At <strong>1</strong>, the large foreground pair and umbrella overlap the street, establishing a strong near plane. At <strong>2</strong>, the building corner and receding curb open a route into depth. Compare that with <strong>3</strong>, the much smaller pedestrians gathered farther back. Overlap, changing scale, and receding edges work together to place you in this space.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>A painting can place a viewer in space through several cooperating choices: overlap, changing scale, placement, and receding edges.</p></div></div>
<div class="credit">Image credit: Gustave Caillebotte, <em>Paris Street; Rainy Day</em>, 1877, Art Institute of Chicago, Charles H. and Mary F. S. Worcester Collection, 1964.336. Public domain/CC0. See the <a href="https://www.artic.edu/artworks/20684/paris-street-rainy-day">Art Institute of Chicago object record</a>.</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</div>

<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="light" style="--acc:#7C4459;--acc-line:#94586E;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#94586E 12%,white)">
<section class="lens" id="lens-light" data-screen-label="painting-light">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><circle cx="20" cy="20" r="6"/><line x1="20" y1="9" x2="20" y2="4"/><line x1="20" y1="31" x2="20" y2="36"/><line x1="9" y1="20" x2="4" y2="20"/><line x1="31" y1="20" x2="36" y2="20"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">02 · Painting</span><h2 class="lens-title">Light: Where does the light send your eye?</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">Light in a painting does more than show what is illuminated. A source, a shaft, and its shadows can give your eye a route through the picture.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Created illustration</span><em>One arrangement · two orders of attention</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Pay attention to light and shadow. How do they impact what you notice first, and how your attention shifts to different parts of each image?</p>
<div class="guide-unit">
<div class="pair">
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:768/1024;background:#211b16;overflow:hidden" role="img" aria-label="Diffuse light arrangement, from the created diptych."><div style="position:absolute;left:0;top:0;width:200%;height:100%"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/painting-light-diptych.jpg" style="width:100%;height:100%;object-fit:cover;object-position:left top" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" style="position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%" aria-hidden="true"><rect class="guide-zone" x="34" y="92" width="682" height="785" rx="12"/><text class="gtext" x="78" y="133">no single route imposed</text></svg></div></div>
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:768/1024;background:#211b16;overflow:hidden" role="img" aria-label="Selective diagonal beam arrangement, from the created diptych, with guide."><div style="position:absolute;left:-100%;top:0;width:200%;height:100%"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/painting-light-diptych.jpg" style="width:100%;height:100%;object-fit:cover;object-position:left top" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" style="position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-zone" d="M1517 48 L1517 306 L1068 744 L1033 571 Z"/><text class="gtext" x="1198" y="107">follow the shaft</text><circle class="marker" cx="1393" cy="207" r="22"/><text class="marker-text" x="1393" y="207" style="font-size:24px">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="1035" cy="694" r="22"/><text class="marker-text" x="1035" y="694" style="font-size:24px">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="1196" cy="737" r="22"/><text class="marker-text" x="1196" y="737" style="font-size:24px">3</text><text class="gtext" x="857" y="917">shadows hold surrounding detail back</text></svg></div></div>
</div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p>On the left, there is no sharp beam telling your eye what to do next. On the right, begin at <strong>1 the entering shaft</strong> and follow it down toward <strong>2 the folded cloth</strong> and <strong>3 the bowl</strong>. Deep shadows make this diagonal route more forceful by holding surrounding detail back.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>Choices about light sources and shadow always impact a viewer's experience of a painting, guiding the eye and emphasizing or minimizing elements of the image.</p></div></div>
<section class="realart" data-screen-label="painting-light-realart">
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3><em>The Calling of Saint Matthew</em></h3><p class="work-meta">Caravaggio · 1599–1600 · oil on canvas</p></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Follow your attention as it moves through the scene. Which faces, hands, or areas of clothing do you notice first? What stays in the darkness?</p>
<div class="guide-unit"><div class="scene natural" data-hist style="background:#191713" role="img" aria-label="Caravaggio's The Calling of Saint Matthew: a bright diagonal zone crosses a group of figures at a table while much of the interior remains dark."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/hist-caravaggio-calling.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 9770 10039" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-zone" d="M9740 2710 L9735 3600 L6520 4870 L3510 6490 L2740 6370 L5580 5080 Z"/><path class="guide-line" d="M6560 4485 C6265 4540 6090 4620 5915 4735"/><path class="guide-line" d="M4415 5310 C4250 5215 4090 5160 3915 5185"/><path class="guide-line" d="M2560 6180 C2760 6250 2925 6305 3130 6365"/><circle class="marker" cx="6180" cy="4560" r="300"/><text class="marker-text" x="6180" y="4560" style="font-size:280px">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="4150" cy="5185" r="300"/><text class="marker-text" x="4150" y="5185" style="font-size:280px">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="2860" cy="6285" r="300"/><text class="marker-text" x="2860" y="6285" style="font-size:280px">3</text></svg></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again at this selected view</span><p>The guide traces a <strong>diagonal zone of brightness</strong>. At <strong>1</strong>, Christ's extended hand and sleeve emerge at its edge. At <strong>2</strong>, brightness gathers on the face and clothing of the seated young man. At <strong>3</strong>, light catches a hand, coins, and the table's edge. The eye can follow this chain of bright events before it accounts for the shadowed room around them.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>Choices about light and shadow can organize attention before a viewer has identified every detail, linking a gesture to particular people and objects while leaving much of the room less distinct.</p></div></div>
<div class="credit">Image credit: Caravaggio, <em>The Calling of Saint Matthew</em>, 1599–1600, Contarelli Chapel, San Luigi dei Francesi, Rome. Public-domain reproduction via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Calling_of_Saint_Matthew-Caravaggo_(1599-1600).jpg">Wikimedia Commons</a>.</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</div>

<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="brushwork" style="--acc:#8E5B6D;--acc-line:#B58193;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#B58193 12%,white)">
<section class="lens" id="lens-brushwork" data-screen-label="painting-brushwork">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><rect x="6" y="6" width="28" height="28"/><rect x="9" y="9" width="22" height="22" stroke-dasharray="1.5 2" opacity=".55"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">03 · Painting</span><h2 class="lens-title">Brushwork: When do you notice the paint itself?</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">The same subject can be handled so smoothly that the image holds together — or with marks that make the painted surface active everywhere.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Created illustration</span><em>One still life · two kinds of handling</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Where does your eye slow, sweep, or change direction? When do you notice the objects, and when do you notice the marks on the surface?</p>
<div class="guide-unit">
<div class="pair">
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="A smooth blue-gray vessel and folded pale cloth on a dark tabletop."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/painting-brushwork-smooth.jpg" alt="" /></div>
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="The same vessel and cloth rendered with visible brush marks across the whole image."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/painting-brushwork-open.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M335 542q95-60 190-10 M350 615q90-45 180 0"/><circle class="marker" cx="442" cy="577" r="24"/><text class="marker-text" x="442" y="577">1</text></svg></div>
</div>
<div class="details" hidden>
<figure><p class="detail-label">Smooth handling · vessel detail</p><div class="detail-crop"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/painting-brushwork-smooth.jpg" alt="Close view of the smooth vessel surface." style="object-position:20% 55%" /></div></figure>
<figure><p class="detail-label">Open handling · vessel detail</p><div class="detail-crop"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/painting-brushwork-open.jpg" alt="Close view of the openly brushed vessel surface." style="object-position:20% 55%" /></div></figure>
</div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p>In the smooth version, transitions across the vessel and cloth keep the still life continuous. In the open version, at <strong>1</strong>, broken curved marks follow the vessel's turn; comparable marks stay visible in the cloth and table. The marks still describe the objects, but also make the surface active.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>Smooth handling can keep attention on a depicted subject; visible directional marks can activate the surface, guide the eye, and make the act of painting part of the experience.</p></div></div>
<section class="realart" data-screen-label="painting-brushwork-realart">
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3><em>Self-Portrait</em></h3><p class="work-meta">Rembrandt van Rijn · 1659 · oil on canvas</p></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Move your eye from the forehead and cheeks to the coat and surrounding field. Where do you mainly see a person, and where do you also notice the paint itself?</p>
<div class="guide-unit"><div class="scene natural" data-hist style="background:#282019" role="img" aria-label="Rembrandt's 1659 Self-Portrait: the face is more brightly modeled and visibly worked than the dark coat and surrounding field."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/hist-rembrandt.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 3090 4000" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-shape" d="M1090 765 C1320 635 1755 642 2030 830 L1970 1775 C1748 1920 1330 1895 1115 1715 Z"/><path class="guide-line" d="M1305 1040 C1445 990 1605 995 1745 1050"/><path class="guide-line" d="M1510 1285 C1600 1335 1690 1418 1745 1518"/><path class="guide-line" d="M2440 1240 C2510 1320 2560 1410 2595 1510"/><circle class="marker" cx="1515" cy="1035" r="88"/><text class="marker-text" x="1515" y="1035" style="font-size:96px">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="1640" cy="1400" r="88"/><text class="marker-text" x="1640" y="1400" style="font-size:96px">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="2530" cy="1390" r="88"/><text class="marker-text" x="2530" y="1390" style="font-size:96px">3</text></svg></div>
<figure class="details" style="display:grid;margin-top:14px" hidden><figcaption class="detail-label">A closer look at the face</figcaption><div class="detail-crop" style="aspect-ratio:1.1132" data-crop-w="757" data-crop-h="680"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/hist-rembrandt.jpg" alt="Close view of Rembrandt's forehead, eyes, and cheek." style="position:absolute;left:-67.37%;top:-23.53%;right:auto;bottom:auto;width:204.1%;height:294.12%;max-width:none;object-fit:fill" /></div></figure>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again at this selected view</span><p>At <strong>1</strong>, short light-and-dark marks stay distinct across the forehead. At <strong>2</strong>, a dense cluster near the nose and cheek makes it hard to ignore the paint as paint. At <strong>3</strong>, the surrounding field reads as quieter handling, letting the head advance. National Gallery of Art conservation research documents richly impasted facial highlights against thinly painted background and hands.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>Brushwork can let a painting form a represented figure while keeping the act and material of painting visible.</p></div></div>
<div class="credit">Image credit: Rembrandt van Rijn, <em>Self-Portrait</em>, 1659, National Gallery of Art, Washington, 1937.1.72. CC0. See the <a href="https://www.nga.gov/artworks/79-self-portrait">National Gallery of Art object record</a>.</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</div>

<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="figure" style="--acc:#75435B;--acc-line:#75435B;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#75435B 12%,white)">
<section class="lens" id="lens-figure" data-screen-label="painting-figure">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><circle cx="14" cy="11" r="3.2"/><path d="M14 15 L14 28 M8 34 L14 28 L20 34"/><circle cx="27" cy="13" r="3.2"/><path d="M27 17 L27 28 M21 34 L27 28 L33 34"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">04 · Painting</span><h2 class="lens-title">Figure: Where do the bodies send your attention?</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">A figure's gaze, gesture, orientation, and distance from another figure can keep attention within a group — or guide it outward.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Created illustration</span><em>Two figures · two attention routes</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Follow the figures' gazes, gestures, and spacing. Who seems connected or isolated? Where do their bodies direct your attention?</p>
<div class="guide-unit">
<div class="pair">
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="A blue-garbed figure and an ochre-garbed figure face one another with hands extended across a modest interval."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/painting-figure-inward.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M466 150 C650 210 870 210 1050 150"/><circle class="marker" cx="774" cy="185" r="24"/><text class="marker-text" x="774" y="185">1</text></svg></div>
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="The same blue-garbed figure faces an ochre-garbed figure who has stepped farther away and turns outward."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/painting-figure-outward.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M1025 150 C1190 180 1320 240 1460 278"/><circle class="marker" cx="1140" cy="210" r="24"/><text class="marker-text" x="1140" y="210">2</text></svg></div>
</div>
<div class="details" hidden><p class="detail-text"><strong>1 · Return.</strong> The facing heads and reaching hands keep the route inside the pair.</p><p class="detail-text"><strong>2 · Depart.</strong> The turned head and outward hand carry the route beyond the picture's edge.</p></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p>In the first image, both figures look and reach across a narrow interval, so your eye can shuttle inside the pair. In the second, the blue figure still turns toward the other, but the ochre figure has stepped away, turned outward, and sent a hand toward the edge. The same two bodies can circulate attention within a group or carry it beyond the frame.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>Pose, gaze, gesture, orientation, and spacing turn figures into relationships, shaping attention and the viewer's sense of connection, isolation, or a route beyond the frame.</p></div></div>
<section class="realart" data-screen-label="painting-figure-realart">
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3><em>The Musicians</em></h3><p class="work-meta">Caravaggio · 1597 · oil on canvas</p></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Before you identify anyone, follow the faces, turned shoulders, hands, and instruments. What keeps your attention moving through the group?</p>
<div class="guide-unit"><div class="scene natural" data-hist style="background:#221a15" role="img" aria-label="Caravaggio's The Musicians: four closely grouped figures, a lute, a violin, and grapes form a web of faces, shoulders, hands, and objects."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/hist-caravaggio-musicians.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 4000 3105" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-zone" d="M1450 315 C1575 210 1845 205 1995 350 C2050 500 2020 735 1900 830 C1740 915 1510 840 1443 675 Z"/><path class="guide-zone" d="M2150 300 C2310 185 2555 230 2665 365 C2705 520 2630 710 2495 770 C2340 805 2200 690 2150 560 Z"/><path class="guide-line" d="M970 1930 C1210 1670 1530 1330 1850 1110"/><path class="guide-line" d="M1525 2825 C1840 2775 2190 2815 2550 2875"/><circle class="marker" cx="2050" cy="175" r="70"/><text class="marker-text" x="2050" y="175" style="font-size:70px">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="1020" cy="2430" r="70"/><text class="marker-text" x="1020" y="2430" style="font-size:70px">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="3340" cy="1220" r="70"/><text class="marker-text" x="3340" y="1220" style="font-size:70px">3</text></svg></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again at the whole painting</span><p>At <strong>1</strong>, two faces near the center turn in different directions, so attention does not settle on one figure. At <strong>2</strong>, the lute and violin create diagonals across the group. At <strong>3</strong>, a bowed head and descending arm return attention toward the grapes. Before a viewer knows the story, orientation, gesture, and nearby objects already connect the figures.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>A figure is read in relation to other figures and objects. Orientation, gesture, spacing, and nearby objects can guide attention through a group before its narrative is understood.</p></div></div>
<div class="credit">Image credit: Caravaggio, <em>The Musicians</em>, 1597, The Metropolitan Museum of Art, Rogers Fund, 1952, 52.81. Public domain. See the <a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/435844">Metropolitan Museum object record</a>.</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</div>

<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="color" style="--acc:#7C4459;--acc-line:#94586E;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#94586E 12%,white)">
<section class="lens" id="lens-color" data-screen-label="painting-value-color">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><rect x="4" y="4" width="32" height="32"/><ellipse cx="20" cy="20" rx="9" ry="7"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">05 · Painting</span><h2 class="lens-title">Value &amp; Color: What does the field make a color do?</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">A color has no single effect. Its relationships with surrounding colors and values can change what comes forward, quiets, or gathers meaning.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Created illustration</span><em>One orange · two color relationships</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Look at the same orange form in both images. How does its surrounding field change what you notice? Which effects come from the visible color relationships, and which meanings depend on context the images do not provide?</p>
<div class="guide-unit">
<div class="pair">
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="An identical orange curved form at the center of a dark blue-gray field."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/painting-color-cool.jpg" alt="" style="position:absolute" /><svg class="orange-fixed" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" style="position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M548 430 C622 366 739 344 861 379 C945 404 992 454 981 512 C971 570 898 622 790 644 C668 670 563 651 519 596 C474 540 489 477 548 430 Z"/></svg><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" aria-hidden="true"><circle class="marker" cx="1015" cy="648" r="24"/><text class="marker-text" x="1015" y="648">1</text></svg></div>
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="The same identical orange curved form at the center of a pale warm cream field."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/painting-color-warm.jpg" alt="" style="position:absolute" /><svg class="orange-fixed" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" style="position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M548 430 C622 366 739 344 861 379 C945 404 992 454 981 512 C971 570 898 622 790 644 C668 670 563 651 519 596 C474 540 489 477 548 430 Z"/></svg><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" aria-hidden="true"><circle class="marker" cx="1015" cy="648" r="24"/><text class="marker-text" x="1015" y="648">2</text></svg></div>
</div>
<div class="details" hidden><p class="detail-text"><strong>1 · Cool / dark field.</strong> Warm-cool and light-dark contrast can make the orange appear luminous, separate, and visually forward.</p><p class="detail-text"><strong>2 · Warm / pale field.</strong> Shared warmth reduces hue contrast; the orange can appear denser and more settled.</p></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p>The orange is digitally identical in both images. Against the deep field, contrast pushes it forward. Against the pale cream, shared warmth settles it while its darker value still distinguishes it. This pair isolates chromatic and spatial effects; emotional and symbolic effects are real but depend on subject, neighboring colors, and cultural context — not on orange alone.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>No color has only one effect. Surrounding color and value can change chromatic intensity and spatial prominence; subject and context shape emotional and symbolic meaning.</p></div></div>
<section class="realart" data-screen-label="painting-value-color-realart">
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3><em>The Bedroom</em></h3><p class="work-meta">Vincent van Gogh · 1889 · oil on canvas</p></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Follow the large wall field, the repeated yellow furniture, and the red coverlet. How do neighboring colors make some areas group together and others interrupt?</p>
<div class="guide-unit"><div class="scene natural" data-hist style="background:#6c93bd" role="img" aria-label="Van Gogh's The Bedroom: blue-appearing walls surround yellow-orange furniture and one red coverlet."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/hist-vangogh.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 2500 1957" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-zone" d="M95 78 L2290 70 L2365 705 L1980 740 L1730 560 L1290 505 L995 680 L520 610 L330 780 L85 660 Z"/><path class="guide-zone" d="M855 735 L1115 650 L1205 1155 L1020 1215 L865 1055 Z"/><path class="guide-zone" d="M1265 835 C1370 775 1515 805 1590 890 L1515 1150 C1405 1188 1305 1135 1250 1020 Z"/><circle class="marker" cx="1870" cy="250" r="70"/><text class="marker-text" x="1870" y="250" style="font-size:70px">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="1010" cy="565" r="70"/><text class="marker-text" x="1010" y="565" style="font-size:70px">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="1445" cy="1035" r="70"/><text class="marker-text" x="1445" y="1035" style="font-size:70px">3</text></svg></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again at the whole painting</span><p>At <strong>1</strong>, the large wall field becomes the room's surrounding color condition. At <strong>2</strong>, related yellow-to-orange colors recur across separate furniture. At <strong>3</strong>, the red coverlet stands out within the yellow-orange bed — its force comes from area and neighboring colors, not a fixed meaning carried by red alone.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>The effect of color depends on relationships: area, repetition, adjacency, and contrast can group colors together, establish a surrounding condition, or interrupt a pattern.</p></div></div>
<div class="credit">Image credit: Vincent van Gogh, <em>The Bedroom</em>, 1889, Art Institute of Chicago, Helen Birch Bartlett Memorial Collection, 1926.417. Public domain/CC0. See the <a href="https://www.artic.edu/artworks/28560/the-bedroom">Art Institute of Chicago object record</a>.</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</div>

<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="materials" style="--acc:#8E5B6D;--acc-line:#B58193;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#B58193 12%,white)">
<section class="lens" id="lens-materials" data-screen-label="painting-materials">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><ellipse cx="16" cy="24" rx="12" ry="9"/><ellipse cx="26" cy="18" rx="10" ry="8"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">06 · Painting</span><h2 class="lens-title">Materials for Paint: What can a layer let you see?</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">Paint can let light pass through a layer so earlier color remains visible, or it can cover what lies beneath — changing how colors combine and an image gathers depth.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Created illustration</span><em>One painted motif · two kinds of coverage</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Look at where color seems to cover the surface and where you can see one layer through another. How does each kind of coverage affect edges, colors, and depth?</p>
<div class="guide-unit">
<div class="pair">
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="A blue-gray band and rust-red oval overlap translucently over a warm ground."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/painting-materials-translucent.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" aria-hidden="true"><circle class="marker" cx="1085" cy="558" r="30"/><text class="marker-text" x="1085" y="558">1</text></svg></div>
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="The same forms use opaque coverage, with a clear red edge covering the blue form at their overlap."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/painting-materials-opaque.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" aria-hidden="true"><circle class="marker" cx="848" cy="416" r="30"/><text class="marker-text" x="848" y="416">2</text></svg></div>
</div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p>In the translucent version, at <strong>1</strong>, the blue and rust layers stay visible through one another, making a darker third color. In the opaque version, at <strong>2</strong>, the red passage covers the blue with a decisive edge. The same motif changes because paint can let earlier color remain active — or block it from view.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>Paint can transmit light through layers or block what lies beneath. Transparency and opacity change how colors combine and whether an image feels layered and luminous or solid and immediate.</p></div></div>
<section class="realart" data-screen-label="painting-materials-realart">
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3><em>Stowing Sail</em></h3><p class="work-meta">Winslow Homer · 1903 · watercolor and graphite on paper</p></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Look closely at the boat's thin lines, color washes, and red strokes near its front. Where do lines remain visible through the washes? Where do colors meet sharply, and where does one drift into another?</p>
<div class="guide-unit"><div class="scene natural" data-hist style="background:#646364" role="img" aria-label="Winslow Homer's Stowing Sail: boats on blue water, with thin dark lines, broad washes, and red strokes near the front of the larger boat."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/hist-homer.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 2500 1586" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M620 910 L1310 650 M1310 650 L1570 750 M340 820 L720 1050"/><path class="guide-zone" d="M630 700 L1330 510 L1450 570 L1290 790 L790 945 L610 870 Z"/><path class="guide-zone" d="M405 1085 C475 1040 585 1045 670 1095 L620 1160 L445 1160 Z"/><circle class="marker" cx="1090" cy="490" r="55"/><text class="marker-text" x="1090" y="490" style="font-size:56px">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="1460" cy="720" r="55"/><text class="marker-text" x="1460" y="720" style="font-size:56px">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="735" cy="1035" r="55"/><text class="marker-text" x="735" y="1035" style="font-size:56px">3</text></svg></div>
<div class="details" hidden>
<figure><div class="detail-crop" style="aspect-ratio:2" data-crop-w="1316" data-crop-h="658"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/hist-homer-contour-wash.jpg" alt="Close view of thin dark contour lines crossing a broad blue-gray wash." style="position:static;width:100%;height:100%;object-fit:cover" /></div><figcaption class="crop-caption"><p class="detail-text"><strong>Contour and wash.</strong> The close view makes the relationship easier to see: thin lines remain legible while a broad wash establishes the larger form.</p><p class="detail-note">The Art Institute documents a graphite underdrawing beneath the watercolor process; this crop shows the visible relation of thin contours and wash, not a material identification for every line.</p></figcaption></figure>
<figure><div class="detail-crop" style="aspect-ratio:2" data-crop-w="746" data-crop-h="373"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/hist-homer-red-blue.jpg" alt="Close view of the red stroke near the front of the boat meeting a blue shadow." style="position:static;width:100%;height:100%;object-fit:cover" /></div><figcaption class="crop-caption"><p class="detail-text"><strong>Red meeting blue.</strong> The Art Institute identifies this red/blue meeting as a heavy red stroke that bled into a still-wet blue shadow.</p><p class="detail-note">At this scale, look for an edge that changes as the two colors enter one another rather than stopping cleanly.</p></figcaption></figure>
</div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again at the whole watercolor</span><p>At <strong>1</strong>, the Art Institute documents graphite underdrawing; thin contour lines remain legible alongside the washes. At <strong>2</strong>, a broad wash carries color across the middle form without a hard edge at every change. At <strong>3</strong>, a heavy red stroke bled upward into a still-wet blue shadow — a record of a wet-on-wet decision, not just a pictured object.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>Materials can let a painting keep different stages visible — planning lines, diluted washes, and colors meeting while still wet. Not every painted edge is a hard boundary.</p></div></div>
<div class="credit">Image credit: Winslow Homer, <em>Stowing Sail</em>, 1903, Art Institute of Chicago, Mr. and Mrs. Martin A. Ryerson Collection, 1933.1252. CC0. Technical observations drawn from the Art Institute's object record. See the <a href="https://www.artic.edu/artworks/16831/stowing-sail">Art Institute of Chicago object record</a>.</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</div>

<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="volume" style="--acc:#4F6B4D;--acc-line:#4F6B4D;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#4F6B4D 12%,white)">
<section class="lens" id="lens-volume" data-screen-label="sculpture-volume">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><line x1="14" y1="4" x2="14" y2="36"/><line x1="26" y1="4" x2="26" y2="36"/><circle cx="20" cy="20" r="6"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">01 · Sculpture</span><h2 class="lens-title">Volume: When does empty space become part of the form?</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">A sculpture does not only shape material. Its boundaries can also shape an opening, bringing surrounding space into the object's form.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Created illustration</span><em>One outer form · two relationships to space</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Pay attention to solid mass and openings. Which form feels more compact? Where does surrounding space become part of its shape?</p>
<div class="guide-unit">
<div class="pair">
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:1672/941;background:linear-gradient(180deg,#3a3733,#221f1c)" role="img" aria-label="A pale rounded monolith on a low plinth against a charcoal wall; its central area is continuous material."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="position:absolute;left:31%;width:38%;bottom:20%;height:auto" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/sculpture-volume-monolith.png" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1000 562" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M445 301 L575 301"/><circle class="marker" cx="510" cy="301" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="510" y="301">1</text></svg></div>
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:1672/941;background:linear-gradient(180deg,#3a3733,#221f1c)" role="img" aria-label="The same monolith with a central arched opening revealing the wall behind it.">
<svg viewBox="0 0 1000 562" style="position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%" aria-hidden="true"><defs><mask id="central-opening" maskUnits="userSpaceOnUse" maskContentUnits="userSpaceOnUse" x="0" y="0" width="1000" height="562"><rect width="1000" height="562" fill="white"/><path d="M462 340 L462 280 C462 235 558 235 558 280 L558 340 Z" fill="black"/></mask></defs><image href="/assets/looking-studio/v2/sculpture-volume-monolith.png" x="310" y="-27" width="380" height="475" mask="url(#central-opening)" preserveAspectRatio="none"/><path d="M462 340 L462 280 C462 235 558 235 558 280 L558 340" fill="none" stroke="rgba(70,61,49,.78)" stroke-width="5"/></svg>
<svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1000 562" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-void" d="M462 340 L462 280 C462 235 558 235 558 280 L558 340 Z"/><circle class="marker" cx="510" cy="300" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="510" y="300">2</text></svg>
</div>
</div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p><strong>1 Continuous material</strong> spans the central area of the first form. In the second, <strong>2 the same outer silhouette surrounds an arched opening</strong> — the wall is visible through that interval, with material remaining below it. This can make the first form feel more concentrated and the second more open to the room around it.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>Choices about mass and void affect how compact, open, visually heavy, or visually light a sculpture appears — and how it relates to the space around it.</p></div></div>
<section class="realart" data-screen-label="sculpture-volume-realart">
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3>Gian Lorenzo Bernini · <em>Apollo and Daphne</em></h3><p class="work-meta">1622–1625 · marble · Galleria Borghese, Rome</p></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Before opening the guide, look for places where the room remains visible within the group. How do those intervals affect the way the bodies and the emerging tree seem to move?</p>
<div class="guide-unit"><div class="scene natural" data-hist style="background:#291c13" role="img" aria-label="Bernini's Apollo and Daphne: Apollo reaches toward Daphne as her raised arm and hair turn into branching laurel; the figures are separated by visible intervals of the room."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/hist-bernini-apollo.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 960 1483" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-void" d="M204 859 C248 775 297 680 340 650 C357 642 369 660 359 682 C326 734 287 824 244 879 C224 900 196 884 204 859 Z"/><path class="guide-void" d="M469 1034 C496 978 529 939 563 942 C584 945 586 970 569 994 C545 1027 522 1074 494 1083 C475 1089 461 1063 469 1034 Z"/><path class="guide-void" d="M580 152 C610 107 643 82 667 98 C686 112 674 141 652 165 C631 189 608 218 586 210 C571 204 570 176 580 152 Z"/><path class="guide-line" d="M447 1030 C474 847 518 657 588 500 C630 406 645 290 633 170"/><circle class="marker" cx="186" cy="840" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="186" y="840" style="font-size:22px">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="448" cy="1053" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="448" y="1053" style="font-size:22px">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="697" cy="122" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="697" y="122" style="font-size:22px">3</text></svg></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again at this selected view</span><p>At <strong>1</strong>, the space beside Apollo's reaching body remains part of the group's silhouette. At <strong>2</strong>, an interval opens between his leg and Daphne's bark-covered form. At <strong>3</strong>, smaller openings break up the branches above. Empty intervals here help make the represented transformation feel as if it opens into the room.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>Open intervals and increasingly thin divisions of marble keep this group from reading as a closed block — volume becomes a way of making a transformation visible.</p></div></div>
<div class="credit">Prototype image credit: Gian Lorenzo Bernini, <em>Apollo and Daphne</em>, 1622–1625, Galleria Borghese, Rome. Photograph by Architas via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0. Production note: Galleria Borghese has authorized this educational use in principle; a formal authorization and institution-supplied photograph are pending and will replace this staging image.</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</div>

<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="scspace" style="--acc:#566F5D;--acc-line:#7B9883;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#7B9883 12%,white)">
<section class="lens" id="lens-scspace" data-screen-label="sculpture-space">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><path d="M6 36 L 6 16 L 20 6 L 34 16 L 34 36 Z"/><path d="M14 36 L 14 24 A 6 6 0 0 1 26 24 L 26 36"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">02 · Sculpture</span><h2 class="lens-title">Space: Why does where you stand matter?</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">Unlike a painting's single framed view, a sculpture is encountered in shared space over time. Moving around it can reveal a different organization of mass and passage.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Created illustration</span><em>One sculpture · two radically different encounters</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Compare the two views. What does the head-on view conceal? What becomes possible to see when you move to the side?</p>
<div class="guide-unit">
<div class="pair">
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="Head-on view of a pale arch-like sculpture that reads as one fused wall with a small opening at its base."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/sculpture-space-fused.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M740 760 C740 720 754 695 785 690 C816 695 830 720 830 760"/><circle class="marker" cx="785" cy="720" r="24"/><text class="marker-text" x="785" y="720">1</text></svg></div>
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="Oblique view of the same sculpture, now visibly made of two tall parallel slabs with a deep passage between them."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/sculpture-space-open.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1536 1024" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M760 120 L800 815 M890 130 L940 820"/><circle class="marker" cx="855" cy="485" r="24"/><text class="marker-text" x="855" y="485">2</text></svg></div>
</div>
<div class="details" hidden><p class="detail-text"><strong>1 · Head-on.</strong> The two slabs align so tightly they read as one wall-like silhouette; the passage is almost entirely concealed.</p><p class="detail-text"><strong>2 · Oblique view.</strong> The slabs separate into a deep channel, and a second, related volume becomes visible for the first time.</p></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p>Head-on, <strong>1 the slabs fuse into one wall-like block</strong> and the passage almost disappears. From the side, <strong>2 the same slabs open into a channel</strong> with a visible far wall, and the object reads as two related volumes rather than one mass. The sculpture has not changed — your position around it has.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>A single view of a sculpture never shows every relation. Where you stand can reveal or conceal mass, passage, and direction — moving around an object is part of encountering it.</p></div></div>
<section class="realart" data-screen-label="sculpture-space-realart">
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3>Gian Lorenzo Bernini · <em>David</em></h3><p class="work-meta">1623–1624 · marble · Galleria Borghese, Rome</p></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Pay attention to what overlaps and what stays separate: an arm crossing the torso, a leg emerging in front of another, small gaps around the hands.</p>
<div class="guide-unit"><div class="scene natural" data-hist style="background:#617286" role="img" aria-label="Bernini's David: the torso twists toward the viewer; a near arm crosses in front of the body, and a bent leg projects toward this view while a straighter leg recedes."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/hist-bernini-david.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1000 714" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M474 142 C462 203 430 283 369 368"/><path class="guide-shape" d="M365 362 C386 340 413 342 425 363 C432 378 414 396 389 397 C368 395 355 381 365 362 Z"/><path class="guide-line" d="M478 268 C501 349 520 441 535 570"/><path class="guide-line" d="M486 281 C458 371 435 463 421 595"/><circle class="marker" cx="352" cy="350" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="352" y="350" style="font-size:22px">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="438" cy="383" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="438" y="383" style="font-size:22px">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="423" cy="484" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="423" y="484" style="font-size:22px">3</text></svg></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again at this selected view</span><p>At <strong>1</strong>, the nearer arm crosses in front of the torso. At <strong>2</strong>, a small interval lets the hands and sling read distinctly. At <strong>3</strong>, the bent leg projects toward this view while the straighter leg recedes. A different position around the sculpture would alter the visible contour and these intervals — not simply reveal the same image from another side.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>This selected view shows how a sculptor can organize a body through overlap, separation, and direction. Viewpoint is part of what becomes visible.</p></div></div>
<div class="credit">Prototype image credit: Gian Lorenzo Bernini, <em>David</em>, 1623–1624, Galleria Borghese, Rome. Photograph by Jk1677 via Wikimedia Commons, CC0. Production note: Galleria Borghese has authorized this educational use in principle; a formal authorization and institution-supplied photograph are pending.</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</div>

<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="setting" style="--acc:#566F5D;--acc-line:#7B9883;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#7B9883 12%,white)">
<section class="lens" id="lens-setting" data-screen-label="sculpture-setting">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><path d="M8 34 L32 34 M20 34 L20 10 M12 22 L28 22"/><circle cx="20" cy="16" r="3"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">03 · Sculpture</span><h2 class="lens-title">Setting: How does a place stage an encounter?</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">A sculpture is never encountered in empty space. Ground, height, enclosure, and light begin to shape your experience before you study the form itself.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Created illustration</span><em>One sculpture · two ways to approach</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> The object is the same in both images. How does its setting change the way you imagine approaching it?</p>
<div class="guide-unit">
<div class="pair">
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="A pale interlocking stone sculpture on a low plinth stands alone on open gray ground in diffuse daylight."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/sculpture-setting-open.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1000 667" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-shape" d="M76 530 C230 486 390 501 500 528 C676 571 793 559 932 515 L932 633 L76 633 Z"/><circle class="marker" cx="502" cy="552" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="502" y="552">1</text></svg></div>
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="The same sculpture stands beyond broad steps, framed by a tall stone threshold and directional daylight."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/sculpture-setting-threshold.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1000 667" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-shape" d="M303 0 L303 618 L696 618 L696 0"/><path class="guide-line" d="M87 608 L508 492 L912 608"/><circle class="marker" cx="508" cy="574" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="508" y="574">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="696" cy="108" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="696" y="108">3</text></svg></div>
</div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p>In the first image, <strong>1 the low base and open ground</strong> leave several paths of approach open. In the second, <strong>2 the steps and centered route</strong> establish distance, while <strong>3 the enclosing threshold and strong light/shadow division</strong> frame it as a destination. The sculpture has not changed, but its setting has staged a different encounter.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>Height, enclosure, light, and approach are part of a sculpture's encounter; they can shape distance, intimacy, authority, and the viewer's movement.</p></div></div>
<section class="realart" data-screen-label="sculpture-setting-realart">
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3><em>Abraham Lincoln</em> in the Lincoln Memorial</h3><p class="work-meta">Daniel Chester French, sculpture completed 1920 · Henry Bacon, architect · selected HABS photograph by Jet Lowe, 1992</p></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Look at the figure, the high base, the broad wall, and the inscription above it. How does this setting change the way the figure meets you?</p>
<div class="guide-unit" id="unit-lincoln"><div class="scene natural" data-hist style="background:#1c1b19" role="img" aria-label="A 1992 HABS photograph of Daniel Chester French's seated Lincoln, high on a broad base, centered within a wall frame and below an inscription."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/hist-lincoln-statue.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 2500 2053" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-zone" d="M680 1465 L1740 1465 L1785 1935 L640 1935 Z"/><path class="guide-line" d="M750 180 L1780 180 M750 180 L680 1780 M1780 180 L1850 1780"/><path class="guide-zone" d="M930 390 L1580 390 L1590 580 L925 580 Z"/><circle class="marker" cx="1835" cy="1675" r="70"/><text class="marker-text" x="1835" y="1675" style="font-size:70px">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="600" cy="1020" r="70"/><text class="marker-text" x="600" y="1020" style="font-size:70px">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="1700" cy="420" r="70"/><text class="marker-text" x="1700" y="420" style="font-size:70px">3</text></svg></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again at this documented 1992 view</span><p>At <strong>1</strong>, the high, broad base separates the body from the viewer's level. At <strong>2</strong>, the symmetrical wall frame encloses and centers the statue. At <strong>3</strong>, the inscription above the figure expands the encounter beyond the carved body. In this view, these relations can create distance, ceremony, and public emphasis.</p></div>
<div class="controls wider-holder" hidden><button class="wider-toggle" type="button" data-role="wider" aria-expanded="false">Pull back to the wider setting</button></div>
<section class="wider-context" hidden>
<h4>Pull back: building, then civic landscape</h4>
<p class="wider-intro">These are separate HABS photographs, not one continuous camera move. Notice what becomes newly visible as the frame widens.</p>
<div class="context-grid">
<figure class="context-card"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/hist-lincoln-building.jpg" alt="A 1991 HABS photograph from the southeast showing the full Lincoln Memorial on a raised platform with columns and lawn." /><figcaption><strong>Building scale.</strong> The raised platform and colonnade show that reaching the sculpture requires crossing an architectural threshold.<span class="image-meta">HABS DC-3, photograph 19 · "View from the southeast" · Jet Lowe · 22 May 1991</span></figcaption></figure>
<figure class="context-card"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/hist-lincoln-aerial.jpg" alt="A 1992 aerial HABS photograph from the west showing the Lincoln Memorial, Reflecting Pool, and the Mall axis toward the Washington Monument." /><figcaption><strong>Civic-landscape scale.</strong> From above, the Memorial becomes one element in a larger civic axis linking bridge, river, roads, and the Washington Monument.<span class="image-meta">HABS DC-3, photograph 7 · "Aerial view from the west" · Jack Boucher · February 1992</span></figcaption></figure>
</div>
</section>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>A sculpture is encountered with its setting. Pedestal, enclosure, symmetry, light, and nearby language can shape whether a viewer experiences an object as intimate, distant, ceremonial, or publicly significant.</p></div></div>
<div class="credit">Image credits: Historic American Buildings Survey, HABS DC-3, Library of Congress: photograph 131 (Jet Lowe, 29 Apr 1992), photograph 19 (Jet Lowe, 22 May 1991), photograph 7 (Jack Boucher, Feb 1992). Daniel Chester French, <em>Abraham Lincoln</em>, completed 1920; Henry Bacon, architect, dedicated 1922. The Library of Congress rights advisory states no known restrictions on images made by the U.S. Government. The three photographs are separately dated documented views. See the <a href="https://www.loc.gov/item/dc0472/">Library of Congress record</a>.</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</div>

<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="body" style="--acc:#4F6B4D;--acc-line:#4F6B4D;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#4F6B4D 12%,white)">
<section class="lens" id="lens-body" data-screen-label="sculpture-body">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><circle cx="20" cy="9" r="4"/><path d="M20 13 L20 26 M20 26 L14 36 M20 26 L28 34"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">04 · Sculpture</span><h2 class="lens-title">Body: How can stillness suggest movement?</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">A standing figure can remain still while its balance makes you feel settled, alert, or ready to shift. Support and counterbalance turn pose into an experience of time.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Created illustration</span><em>One figure · two ways to carry weight</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Where does each body seem to carry its weight? Which pose feels settled, and which seems to anticipate action?</p>
<div class="guide-unit">
<div class="pair">
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="A draped standing figure faces forward on a low plinth; both feet are evenly spaced and the shoulders and hips appear level."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/sculpture-body-even.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1000 667" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M440 140 L595 140"/><path class="guide-line" d="M447 278 L590 278"/><circle class="marker" cx="414" cy="370" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="414" y="370">1</text></svg></div>
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="The same figure stands with weight shifted onto one leg; the other foot rests farther out."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/sculpture-body-shifted.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1000 667" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M425 150 L574 137"/><path class="guide-line" d="M510 280 L471 604"/><path class="guide-line" d="M532 286 C560 456 574 523 615 603"/><circle class="marker" cx="471" cy="505" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="471" y="505">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="614" cy="565" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="614" y="565">3</text></svg></div>
</div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p>In the first figure, <strong>1 the body stays centered between two planted feet;</strong> shoulders and hips remain nearly level. In the second, <strong>2 the body settles over one supporting leg,</strong> and <strong>3 the relaxed leg and opposing tilts of hips and shoulders</strong> keep the figure balanced while making its stillness feel responsive.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>Weight and counterbalance can make a fixed body appear stable, relaxed, tense, or ready to move. Pose shapes the viewer's sense of action and time.</p></div></div>
<section class="realart" data-screen-label="sculpture-body-realart">
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3><em>Pair of Standing Nude Male Figures</em></h3><p class="work-meta">Auguste Rodin · ca. 1911 · terracotta</p></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Compare the two figures from head to base. Where does each body lean or bend?</p>
<div class="guide-unit"><div class="scene natural" data-hist style="background:#332f2b" role="img" aria-label="Rodin's pair of terracotta standing nude male figures: the left figure curves sideways above a broad base; the right stands more upright with a tilted head and raised arm."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/hist-rodin-pair.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 4000 3571" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M1450 550 C1230 810 1350 1160 1420 1450 C1480 1690 1320 1880 1090 2110 C890 2310 890 2570 1110 2860"/><path class="guide-line" d="M2640 560 C2470 920 2530 1280 2590 1630 C2630 1960 2555 2320 2500 2870"/><path class="guide-line" d="M2560 700 C2780 600 3000 765 3090 1030 C3160 1185 3100 1340 3000 1430"/><circle class="marker" cx="960" cy="1810" r="70"/><text class="marker-text" x="960" y="1810" style="font-size:70px">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="2320" cy="1820" r="70"/><text class="marker-text" x="2320" y="1820" style="font-size:70px">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="3260" cy="990" r="70"/><text class="marker-text" x="3260" y="990" style="font-size:70px">3</text></svg></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again at the whole pair</span><p>At <strong>1</strong>, the left figure's head, shoulder, torso, and folded lower body make a long sideward sweep. At <strong>2</strong>, the right figure follows a more upright path toward the base. At <strong>3</strong>, its tilted head and raised arm cut across that path. In both figures, the parts do not line up into one fixed axis; their shifts make the poses appear to adjust as you look.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>When a sculptor shifts and counters the parts of a body, a pose can suggest balance in progress. Even a still figure can make a viewer sense adjustment or possible movement.</p></div></div>
<div class="credit">Image credit: Auguste Rodin, <em>Pair of Standing Nude Male Figures Demonstrating the Principles of Contrapposto according to Michelangelo and Phidias</em>, ca. 1911, The Metropolitan Museum of Art, Gift of Iris and B. Gerald Cantor Foundation, 1987, 1987.179.1, .2. Public domain. See the <a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/207693">Metropolitan Museum object record</a>.</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</div>

<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="material" style="--acc:#4E6B50;--acc-line:#5F8060;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#5F8060 12%,white)">
<section class="lens" id="lens-material" data-screen-label="sculpture-material">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><path d="M10 34 C10 14 30 14 30 34 Z"/><line x1="15" y1="34" x2="15" y2="20" stroke-dasharray="2 2"/><line x1="25" y1="34" x2="25" y2="20" stroke-dasharray="2 2"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">05 · Sculpture</span><h2 class="lens-title">Material: How does matter change form?</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">Material is not a surface placed over a finished idea. Its properties shape connections, spans, edges, and the kinds of forms a sculptor can bring into view.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Created illustration</span><em>A related form · two material solutions</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> The basic relationship is similar, but the materials differ. How does each material seem to shape the form?</p>
<div class="guide-unit">
<div class="pair">
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="A pale limestone sculpture with a thick continuous arch, a tall opening, and a lower circular mass stands on a plinth."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/sculpture-material-stone.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1000 667" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M520 64 C640 66 711 154 711 292 L711 520"/><circle class="marker" cx="675" cy="270" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="675" y="270">1</text></svg></div>
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="A blackened steel construction with slender rods, a large open oval, and visible welded joints stands on the same plinth."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/sculpture-material-metal.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1000 667" aria-hidden="true"><circle class="guide-shape" cx="404" cy="126" r="28"/><path class="guide-line" d="M665 244 C705 374 696 500 660 548"/><circle class="marker" cx="369" cy="109" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="369" y="109">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="721" cy="383" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="721" y="383">3</text></svg></div>
</div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p>In the stone solution, at <strong>1</strong>, broad continuous material carries the arch around the opening. In the metal construction, at <strong>2</strong>, distinct rods meet at visible joints, and at <strong>3</strong>, slender members span larger open intervals. The same relationship produces a different kind of form: concentrated continuity in one, extension and connection in the other.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>Material is not a covering placed over form. Its weight, resistance, flexibility, and grain shape possible forms; its texture and sheen affect a viewer's sense of mass, delicacy, and permanence.</p></div></div>
<section class="realart" data-screen-label="sculpture-material-realart">
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3><em>Torso</em></h3><p class="work-meta">Auguste Rodin · modeled ca. 1877–78, cast 1979 · bronze</p></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Follow where light catches the torso and where it falls into dark recesses. Then attend to the uneven, interrupted edges.</p>
<div class="guide-unit"><div class="scene natural" data-hist style="background:#7b7d80" role="img" aria-label="A fragmentary bronze torso by Rodin: strong highlights and dark recesses describe the chest, shoulder areas, and uneven outer edges."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/hist-rodin-torso.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 2734 4000" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-shape" d="M1160 800 C1294 674 1533 668 1645 798 C1682 945 1645 1184 1592 1400 C1515 1537 1288 1544 1164 1392 C1104 1207 1089 977 1160 800 Z"/><path class="guide-line" d="M1770 540 C1870 642 1940 755 1962 872 C1995 1010 1980 1138 1930 1265"/><path class="guide-line" d="M1744 2495 C1870 2526 1955 2620 1968 2738 C1978 2845 1905 2920 1804 2958"/><circle class="marker" cx="1350" cy="1080" r="55"/><text class="marker-text" x="1350" y="1080" style="font-size:56px">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="1905" cy="855" r="55"/><text class="marker-text" x="1905" y="855" style="font-size:56px">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="1920" cy="2740" r="55"/><text class="marker-text" x="1920" y="2740" style="font-size:56px">3</text></svg></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again at this selected view</span><p>At <strong>1</strong>, light collects on the chest's rounded planes, making their form easier to follow. At <strong>2</strong>, bright ridges and dark recesses sit close together across the shoulder surface. At <strong>3</strong>, the silhouette breaks into an uneven ending. The bronze's shifting reflections and dark pockets make this fragment read as both a body and a material object.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>Material is not an inert finish. This bronze's uneven relief, shifting highlights, and fragmentary boundaries shape how the body's mass is experienced.</p></div></div>
<div class="credit">Image credit: Auguste Rodin, <em>Torso</em>, modeled ca. 1877–78, cast 1979 by Coubertin Foundry, The Metropolitan Museum of Art, Gift of Iris and B. Gerald Cantor Foundation, 1984, 1984.364.1. Public domain. See the <a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/207484">Metropolitan Museum object record</a>.</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</div>

<div class="lens-wrap is-hidden" data-lens="making" style="--acc:#4E6B50;--acc-line:#5F8060;--acc-soft:color-mix(in oklab,#5F8060 12%,white)">
<section class="lens" id="lens-making" data-screen-label="sculpture-making">
<div class="lens-head"><span class="lens-sig" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 40 40"><rect x="6" y="18" width="28" height="16"/><path d="M18 6 L 22 6 L 20 14 Z"/><line x1="20" y1="14" x2="20" y2="18"/></svg></span><div><span class="lens-idx">06 · Sculpture</span><h2 class="lens-title">Making: What traces can an object keep?</h2></div></div>
<p class="lens-cue">A sculpture can preserve signs of decisions: an interrupted plane, an added volume, a seam, or a connector — letting viewers encounter form as something made.</p>
<div class="study">
<div class="study-head"><span class="study-label">Created illustration</span><em>A related form · two kinds of visible evidence</em></div>
<div class="study-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Where do you see uninterrupted material? Where do you see a flat interruption, a seam, or a connection?</p>
<div class="guide-unit">
<div class="pair">
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="A pale abstract sculpture interrupted by one broad diagonal flat plane."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/sculpture-making-cut.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1000 667" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M365 573 L634 166"/><circle class="marker" cx="529" cy="350" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="529" y="350">1</text></svg></div>
<div class="scene" style="aspect-ratio:3/2" role="img" aria-label="A related sculpture built from separate volumes with dark short connectors crossing seams between them."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/sculpture-making-assembled.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 1000 667" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-line" d="M355 448 C436 465 500 445 544 397"/><circle class="guide-shape" cx="496" cy="268" r="25"/><circle class="marker" cx="337" cy="441" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="337" y="441">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="523" cy="267" r="20"/><text class="marker-text" x="523" y="267">3</text></svg></div>
</div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again</span><p>In the first sculpture, at <strong>1</strong>, a broad planar facet cuts across the surrounding rounded surfaces, suggesting material was removed. In the second, at <strong>2</strong>, separate volumes meet along seams, and at <strong>3</strong>, short dark connectors cross those boundaries — evidence of addition and joining. Visible traces can point to decisions without giving a complete record of how an object was made.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway concept</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway concept</div><p>Cuts, additions, seams, and joins preserve evidence of making. They let viewers see form as the result of decisions, while never revealing the complete process by themselves.</p></div></div>
<section class="realart" data-screen-label="sculpture-making-realart">
<div class="ra-head"><div><span class="ra-kicker">In Real Art</span><h3><em>Unfinished Statuette</em></h3><p class="work-meta">Middle Kingdom, Dynasty 12, Egypt · ca. 1980–1975 BCE · limestone and ink</p></div></div>
<div class="ra-body">
<p class="look"><strong>Look first.</strong> Notice where the stone is rough or irregular, where it becomes smoother, and where red ink appears.</p>
<div class="guide-unit"><div class="scene natural" data-hist style="background:#b6b6b2" role="img" aria-label="An unfinished limestone statuette: the smoother left-central face bears a red ink outline of a standing figure; the upper and right edges remain rough."><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" src="/assets/looking-studio/v2/hist-statuette.jpg" alt="" /><svg class="gsvg" viewBox="0 0 2642 3889" aria-hidden="true"><path class="guide-shape" d="M822 572 C1082 447 1586 491 1700 728 L1681 3158 C1449 3281 1025 3252 858 3090 Z"/><path class="guide-line" d="M1560 590 C1680 780 1670 1180 1648 1532 C1626 2150 1692 2790 1588 3290"/><path class="guide-line" d="M1088 791 C1190 942 1310 1074 1422 1191 C1510 1288 1517 1472 1438 1578 C1335 1690 1300 1820 1325 1992 L1383 2795"/><circle class="marker" cx="1050" cy="1840" r="55"/><text class="marker-text" x="1050" y="1840" style="font-size:56px">1</text><circle class="marker" cx="1575" cy="2000" r="55"/><text class="marker-text" x="1575" y="2000" style="font-size:56px">2</text><circle class="marker" cx="1035" cy="970" r="55"/><text class="marker-text" x="1035" y="970" style="font-size:56px">3</text></svg></div>
<div class="controls"><button class="guide-toggle" type="button" data-role="guide" aria-pressed="false"><span class="gdot" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="gt-label">Show visual guide</span></button></div>
<div class="caption" hidden><span class="cap-label">Look again at this selected view</span><p>At <strong>1</strong>, a broad smoother central field differs from the irregular stone around it. At <strong>2</strong>, rougher material remains along the side and upper edge. At <strong>3</strong>, red ink lines describe a standing figure across the smoother face. These contrasts give visible evidence of different surface conditions and an unfinished carved state, without telling us the exact sequence of work.</p></div>
</div>
<div class="takeaway-wrap"><div class="controls"><button class="takeaway-toggle" type="button" data-role="takeaway" aria-expanded="false">Show takeaway connection</button></div><div class="takeaway" hidden><div class="tk-lbl">Takeaway connection</div><p>Contrasting surfaces and a drawn contour make this object's partial state visible. Traces can help a viewer read a sculpture as the result of decisions and actions, without providing a complete record of its making.</p></div></div>
<div class="credit">Image credit: <em>Unfinished Statuette</em>, ca. 1980–1975 BCE, limestone and ink, The Metropolitan Museum of Art, Rogers Fund and Edward S. Harkness Gift, 1920, 20.3.164. Public domain. See the <a href="https://www.metmuseum.org/art/collection/search/562009">Metropolitan Museum object record</a>.</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</div>

</div>
